<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kadam school</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Kadampa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kadam school was a tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> founded in the eleventh century by Dromtön, a disciple of the Indian teacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atisha">Atisha</a>, who established Reting Monastery in 1056. It stressed monastic discipline and the graded path to enlightenment. Its teachings were absorbed into the other schools, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsongkhapa">Tsongkhapa</a>, sometimes called the New Kadam.</p></div>
